      content: 'A 40-year-old right-hand-dominant female graphic designer presents to the PM&R clinic with an 8-month history of chronic right-sided neck and shoulder pain. She describes a constant deep ache in her right upper trapezius and periscapular region with intermittent sharp pain that "shoots" up the right side of her neck into the base of her skull, causing tension-type headaches 3-4 times per week. The pain worsens throughout the workday and with stress. She works 8-10 hours per day at a computer using a drawing tablet. She has tried ibuprofen, heating pads, and a brief course of physical therapy (6 visits) without lasting improvement. She is convinced she has "a pinched nerve" and has been requesting an MRI from her primary care physician, who referred her to PM&R instead.',
