    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characteristics**: Location (right upper trapezius, levator scapulae, suboccipital region). Quality (deep ache with referred pain pattern). Temporal pattern (worse with sustained postures, stress, and by end of day). Aggravating factors (computer work, emotional stress, cold exposure, poor sleep). Relieving factors (heat, massage, position change, weekends/vacations).\n- **Referred pain pattern**: Upper trapezius trigger points classically refer pain to the posterolateral neck, temporal region, and behind the eye — "question mark" pattern. Does her headache pattern match this?\n- **Sleep quality**: Poor sleep is both a consequence and perpetuating factor of MPS. Assess sleep position, quality, and duration. Non-restorative sleep is common.\n- **Psychosocial assessment**: Stress level, anxiety (GAD-7), depression (PHQ-9), work satisfaction, perfectionism traits, catastrophizing (Pain Catastrophizing Scale). These are critical perpetuating factors.\n- **Widespread pain screening**: Does she have pain in other body regions? More than 3 months? Widespread Pain Index and Symptom Severity Scale — screen for fibromyalgia if pain is diffuse.\n- **Prior PT details**: What was done in the 6 sessions? Was trigger point therapy specifically addressed? Was postural correction included? Brief, non-specific PT is often inadequate for MPS.\n- **Ergonomic history**: Workstation setup, breaks, posture habits, use of drawing tablet (asymmetric arm positioning).\n- **Exercise habits**: Current activity level, fitness, stretching routine.\n- **Cervical spine symptoms**: Any radicular symptoms (numbness, tingling, weakness in a dermatomal pattern)? Important to differentiate from radiculopathy.',
