      content: '**Vitals**: BP 122/76 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, Temp 36.8C, BMI 23.\n\n**Postural Assessment**:\n- Forward head posture (ear anterior to shoulder line by approximately 3 cm)\n- Rounded shoulders bilaterally, right worse than left\n- Mild right scapular protraction with winging\n- Right shoulder elevated approximately 1 cm compared to left (upper trapezius hypertonicity)\n\n**Cervical Spine Examination**:\n- **ROM**: Flexion 45 degrees (normal), extension 50 degrees (normal), right lateral flexion 30 degrees (mildly limited by tightness, not pain), left lateral flexion 40 degrees (normal), rotation symmetric at 70 degrees bilaterally.\n- **Spurling test**: Negative bilaterally — no radicular symptoms provoked.\n- **Lhermitte sign**: Negative.\n\n**Trigger Point Examination** (the key diagnostic finding):\n- **Right upper trapezius**: Palpable taut band with a discrete, exquisitely tender **trigger point** (TrP) in the mid-belly of the muscle. Firm sustained pressure reproduces her typical neck pain AND her headache pattern (referred pain to the right temporal region). Local twitch response elicited on snapping palpation.\n- **Right levator scapulae**: Active TrP at the superior angle of the scapula. Referred pain to the right lateral neck.\n- **Right suboccipital muscles**: Active TrP in the right rectus capitis posterior major. Referred pain to the right occipital region — consistent with her tension headache pattern.\n- **Right infraspinatus**: Latent TrP (tender on palpation but does not spontaneously cause pain).\n- **Left upper trapezius**: Mild taut bands without active TrPs.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Motor**: 5/5 throughout bilateral upper and lower extremities. No weakness in any myotomal distribution.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ and symmetric throughout (biceps, triceps, brachioradialis, patellar, Achilles). No pathologic reflexes.\n- **Sensation**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, and vibration in all dermatomes. No dermatomal sensory loss.\n\n**Shoulder exam**: Full ROM bilaterally. Negative impingement signs. Rotator cuff strength 5/5 bilaterally.',
