    const Section(
      title: '+ How do you differentiate myofascial pain syndrome from fibromyalgia and cervical radiculopathy? What are the diagnostic criteria for MPS?',
      content: '**Myofascial Pain Syndrome (MPS) Diagnostic Features**:\n\nMPS is a clinical diagnosis based on physical examination findings. There is no confirmatory blood test or imaging study. The key diagnostic features are:\n\n1. **Taut band**: A palpable, ropy, tense band of muscle fibers within a skeletal muscle\n2. **Trigger point (TrP)**: A hyperirritable spot within the taut band that is painful on compression\n3. **Referred pain pattern**: Pressure on the TrP reproduces the patient\'s recognized pain pattern in a predictable, non-dermatomal distribution\n4. **Local twitch response**: A visible or palpable brief contraction of the taut band when the TrP is snapped or needled\n5. **Restricted ROM**: Due to taut band shortening of the muscle\n\n**Active vs. Latent Trigger Points**:\n- **Active TrP**: Spontaneously painful and reproduces the patient\'s clinical pain complaint. Causes referred pain at rest or with movement.\n- **Latent TrP**: Painful only when palpated. Does not cause spontaneous pain but may restrict ROM and cause weakness. Can become active under stress.\n\n**Differentiating MPS from Fibromyalgia**:\n\n| Feature | Myofascial Pain Syndrome | Fibromyalgia |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain distribution | Regional (one or few muscles) | Widespread (all four body quadrants) |\n| Key finding | Trigger points in taut bands | Widespread allodynia, tender points (though 2010 criteria use WPI/SSS instead) |\n| Referred pain | Yes — specific patterns | Not a primary feature |\n| Twitch response | Present | Absent |\n| Fatigue | Mild, localized | Severe, central |\n| Sleep disturbance | Secondary to pain | Primary (non-restorative sleep is a core feature) |\n| Cognitive symptoms | Minimal | "Fibro fog" — prominent |\n| Central sensitization | Mild/absent | Prominent |\n| Prognosis | Usually resolves with treatment | Chronic, waxing-waning |\n\n**However**: MPS and fibromyalgia can coexist. Chronic untreated MPS with persistent nociceptive input may lead to central sensitization and evolve into fibromyalgia in susceptible individuals.\n\n**Differentiating MPS from Cervical Radiculopathy**:\n\n| Feature | Myofascial Pain Syndrome | Cervical Radiculopathy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain pattern | Non-dermatomal, follows TrP referral maps | Dermatomal (follows specific nerve root) |\n| Numbness/tingling | Absent | Present in dermatomal distribution |\n| Weakness | Muscle shortening/guarding (not true neurologic weakness) | True motor weakness in myotomal pattern |\n| Reflexes | Normal | Diminished at involved level |\n| Spurling test | Negative | Positive |\n| EMG/NCS | Normal | Abnormal (fibrillations, reduced recruitment) |\n| Exam finding | Trigger points with referred pain | Radicular signs (weakness, sensory loss, reflex change) |\n\n**This patient**: Regional pain (right neck/shoulder), trigger points with characteristic referred pain patterns reproducing her symptoms, normal neurologic exam, negative Spurling test — classic MPS, not radiculopathy. An MRI is NOT indicated.\n\n**When WOULD imaging be indicated in MPS patients**:\n- Neurologic deficits on exam\n- Positive Spurling or radicular signs\n- Red flags (trauma, cancer history, progressive symptoms)\n- Failure to respond to 8-12 weeks of appropriate MPS-targeted treatment',
