      title: '+ Outline your treatment plan including trigger point interventions, physical therapy, and stress management.',
      content: '**Comprehensive MPS Treatment Plan**:\n\n**1. Trigger Point Interventions (In-Office)**:\n\n**Trigger Point Injection (TPI)**:\n- **Technique**: Identify TrP by palpation. Insert 25-gauge needle into the TrP using a fast-in, fan-out technique to elicit local twitch responses. Inject 1-2 mL per TrP site.\n- **Injectate options**:\n  - **Lidocaine 1% (without epinephrine)**: Most commonly used. Provides immediate pain relief and breaks the pain cycle. Low risk.\n  - **Bupivacaine 0.25%**: Longer-acting local anesthetic.\n  - **Botulinum toxin type A**: Reserved for refractory cases. Provides longer-lasting relief (3-4 months) by reducing muscle contracture. Not first-line due to cost.\n- **Today\'s plan**: Inject the right upper trapezius and right levator scapulae active TrPs with 1% lidocaine. Treat 2-3 TrPs per session maximum.\n\n**Dry Needling**:\n- Insertion of a solid filament needle (acupuncture needle) into the TrP without injectate\n- Elicits local twitch responses that release the taut band\n- Evidence suggests dry needling is equally effective to wet needling for many patients\n- Often performed by specially trained physical therapists (scope varies by state)\n\n**Post-injection protocol**: Apply moist heat or ice. Perform gentle stretching of the treated muscle immediately. Avoid strenuous use of the muscle for 24-48 hours. TPI can be repeated every 2-4 weeks for 3-4 sessions.\n\n**2. Physical Therapy Program (MPS-Specific)**:\n- **Manual therapy**: Myofascial release, ischemic compression of TrPs, strain-counterstrain technique.\n- **Stretching**: Upper trapezius stretch (lateral neck flexion with scapular depression), levator scapulae stretch (chin toward axilla), suboccipital release stretches. Hold 30 seconds, 3-5 repetitions, 3 times daily.\n- **Postural correction**: Chin tuck exercises (deep neck flexor strengthening), scapular retraction and depression exercises, thoracic extension mobilization. Address the forward head posture and rounded shoulders.\n- **Strengthening**: Lower and middle trapezius strengthening (prone Y, T, W exercises), serratus anterior activation, deep neck flexor endurance training. The goal is to shift the load away from the overactive upper trapezius.\n- **Aerobic exercise**: 30 minutes of moderate aerobic exercise 5 days per week. Walking, cycling, or swimming. Aerobic exercise raises endorphin levels, improves sleep, and reduces central pain sensitization.\n- **Frequency**: 2 times per week for 8-12 weeks with emphasis on daily home exercise program.\n\n**3. Stress Management (Critical Perpetuating Factor)**:\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)**: Address catastrophizing, fear-avoidance beliefs, and pain-related anxiety. Reframe her belief that she has a "pinched nerve" (see Domain E).\n- **Relaxation techniques**: Progressive muscle relaxation (PMR), diaphragmatic breathing, mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR).\n- **Biofeedback**: Surface EMG biofeedback can teach the patient to reduce upper trapezius muscle tension during rest and work. Highly effective for MPS.\n- **Sleep hygiene**: Consistent sleep/wake times, cool dark room, no screens 1 hour before bed, limit caffeine after noon. If sleep remains poor, consider low-dose amitriptyline (10-25 mg at bedtime) which also has analgesic properties for chronic pain.\n\n**4. Pharmacologic Adjuncts**:\n- **Topical therapy**: Diclofenac gel 1% applied to the upper trapezius 4 times daily. Minimal systemic absorption.\n- **Muscle relaxant**: Tizanidine 2-4 mg at bedtime — reduces muscle tone centrally and promotes sleep. Preferable to cyclobenzaprine for chronic use.\n- **Low-dose tricyclic**: Amitriptyline 10-25 mg at bedtime if sleep disruption and daily headaches are prominent. Addresses pain, sleep, and headache simultaneously.\n- **Avoid**: Chronic NSAID use (GI, renal, cardiovascular risks), opioids (not effective for MPS, risk of dependence), benzodiazepines.\n\n**5. Self-Management Tools**:\n- Foam roller and tennis ball for self-myofascial release\n- Theracane or similar self-massage device for reaching upper trapezius\n- Hot pack or microwavable heat wrap for home use\n- Stretching app or video for daily guided stretching routine',
