      title: '+ How would you implement ergonomic modifications and a biopsychosocial model for this patient\'s management?',
      content: '**Ergonomic Consultation**:\n\n1. **Referral**: Request a formal ergonomic assessment of her workstation through occupational therapy or a certified ergonomic consultant. Many employers will cover this, especially if documented as medically necessary.\n\n2. **Drawing Tablet-Specific Modifications**:\n   - Position the drawing tablet directly in front of her at desk height (not off to one side — asymmetric positioning creates unilateral trapezius overload).\n   - Tilt the tablet at a slight angle using a tablet stand to reduce neck flexion.\n   - Alternate between tablet and mouse/keyboard tasks to reduce sustained static postures.\n   - Consider a larger tablet that allows elbow-driven movements rather than wrist-only movements.\n\n3. **General Workstation Modifications**:\n   - Monitor at eye level, directly in front\n   - Chair with adequate lumbar support and adjustable armrests\n   - Standing desk option for postural variation (alternate sitting and standing every 30-45 minutes)\n   - Headset for phone use\n   - Document holder at monitor level\n\n4. **Behavioral Workplace Interventions**:\n   - Timed micro-breaks every 30 minutes (stretch, walk, change position)\n   - Workplace stretching program posted at her desk\n   - Stress awareness — recognize when trapezius is tensing during stressful tasks and consciously relax\n\n**Biopsychosocial Model Implementation**:\n\nChronic MPS is a prototypical biopsychosocial condition where biological, psychological, and social factors all perpetuate the pain cycle. A purely biomedical approach (injections alone) will fail without addressing all three domains.\n\n**Biological Factors**:\n- Peripheral muscle dysfunction (trigger points, taut bands)\n- Postural biomechanics (forward head posture, scapular dyskinesis)\n- Physical deconditioning\n- Sleep disruption\n- Treatment: TPI, PT, exercise, ergonomics, pharmacology\n\n**Psychological Factors**:\n- Pain catastrophizing ("I have a pinched nerve — something is seriously wrong")\n- Fear-avoidance beliefs (avoiding movement for fear of worsening damage)\n- Anxiety and work-related stress\n- Perfectionism traits (common in graphic designers — may resist taking breaks)\n- Loss of self-efficacy (feeling helpless about the pain)\n- Treatment: CBT, pain neuroscience education, relaxation training, biofeedback, sports psychology or pain psychology referral\n\n**Social Factors**:\n- Work demands (long hours, deadlines, sustained postures)\n- Work culture (is taking breaks acceptable? supportive supervisor?)\n- Financial pressure to work through pain\n- Reduced social and recreational activities due to pain\n- Treatment: Ergonomic modifications, workplace advocacy, activity pacing, social reconnection\n\n**Interdisciplinary Team**:\n- PM&R physician: Overall management, injections, medical coordination\n- Physical therapist: Manual therapy, exercise prescription, postural correction\n- Psychologist: CBT, stress management, biofeedback\n- Occupational therapist: Ergonomic assessment, activity modification\n- Primary care: Coordinate general health, diabetes screening (BMI 23 is fine here, but address general wellness)\n\n**Outcome Measures to Track**:\n- Visual Analog Scale (VAS) for pain\n- Neck Disability Index (NDI)\n- Headache frequency diary\n- PHQ-9 and GAD-7 for mood\n- Sleep quality (Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index)\n- Work productivity measures\n- Patient-Specific Functional Scale (PSFS)',
