    const Section(
      title: '+ Demonstrate how you would redirect this patient\'s expectations about her diagnosis while validating her concerns.',
      content: 'I would respond with respect for her perspective while firmly and kindly educating:\n\n**1. Validate her pain and her concern**:\n\'I want to be very clear about something — I am taking your pain extremely seriously. Eight months of pain that is affecting your ability to work and causing headaches multiple times per week is significant, and you deserve a thorough evaluation and effective treatment. That is exactly what we are doing today.\'\n\n**2. Demonstrate the evidence from the exam**:\n\'Let me show you what I found on your exam and why I am confident in this diagnosis. When I pressed on this spot in your trapezius muscle — right here — it reproduced the exact pain you have been experiencing, including the headache. That is called a trigger point, and the fact that pressing on a specific muscle point recreated your typical pain pattern tells us where the pain generator is. Your neurologic exam is completely normal — your reflexes are symmetric, your strength is full, you have no numbness or tingling in a nerve pattern, and the Spurling test that specifically provokes pinched nerves was negative. If you had a pinched nerve, I would expect to find abnormalities in at least one of those areas.\'\n\n**3. Reframe "just muscle pain" respectfully**:\n\'I also want to address the idea that this is "just" muscle pain. Myofascial pain syndrome is a legitimate, well-studied medical condition — it is not the same as a simple muscle strain that goes away in a few days. Trigger points involve dysfunctional muscle tissue that develops its own cycle of pain, spasm, and referred symptoms. The headaches you are having are caused by these trigger points referring pain to your head. This is real pathology, and it requires real treatment.\'\n\n**4. Address the MRI request directly**:\n\'Regarding the MRI — I completely understand why you want one. An MRI feels like a definitive answer, and your friend\'s experience makes you worry you might have the same thing. Here is my honest assessment: based on your normal neurologic exam, negative Spurling test, and the fact that your pain is clearly reproduced by trigger point palpation rather than nerve provocation, an MRI right now would very likely be normal — or it might show common age-related findings that look abnormal but are not causing your symptoms. In fact, studies show that 30-40% of people your age with NO neck pain have disc herniations on MRI. An incidental finding could actually lead us down the wrong treatment path.\'\n\n**5. Offer a conditional plan**:\n\'Here is what I propose: let us treat the condition we have identified with targeted interventions — trigger point injections, proper physical therapy focused on your specific trigger points and posture, ergonomic changes, and stress management. If after 8-12 weeks of this comprehensive approach you are not significantly better, I will absolutely order an MRI at that point. That is not dismissing your concern — it is applying a logical diagnostic and treatment sequence.\'\n\n**6. Empower her with understanding**:\n\'I would actually like you to learn more about myofascial pain syndrome. When you understand the mechanism — how these trigger points develop from sustained postures and stress, and how they refer pain in predictable patterns — it often gives patients a sense of control over their condition. Unlike a disc herniation where you might feel helpless, MPS is a condition where your active participation in stretching, posture correction, stress management, and ergonomics can make a dramatic difference.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validating pain severity, demonstrating exam findings as evidence, reframing "muscle pain" as legitimate pathology, educating about MRI limitations, offering a conditional plan that honors her concern, empowering through knowledge, avoiding defensive posturing.',
